<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nabeshima clan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nabeshima were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> family of Hizen province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> who ruled the Saga domain throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. Originally chief retainers of the Ryuzoji, they took over their lords' lands under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naoshige">Nabeshima Naoshige</a> after 1584. The family sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arita">Arita</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naomasa">Nabeshima Naomasa</a> in the 1850s, Western industrial technology, and Saga men played a leading part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabeshima-clan.jpg" alt="Nabeshima clan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mukai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Crest_Nabesima_Gyouyou.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The early origins of the family are uncertain, and its genealogies give two accounts. One traces it to the Uda Genji through the Sasaki clan: Nagaoka Tsunehide, a descendant of the Sasaki, settled at Nabeshima in Hizen and took the place-name as his surname. The other links the family with the Shōni clan, which claimed descent from Fujiwara no Hidesato, a Shōni member having succeeded to the Nabeshima line through his mother's relatives.</p><p>By the 16th century the Nabeshima were bound to the Ryūzōji by marriage as well as service. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naoshige">Nabeshima Naoshige</a>, born in 1538 at the family residence in Honjō, was a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryuzoji-takanobu">Ryūzōji Takanobu</a> through his mother and became his stepbrother when Takanobu's mother, Keigin-ni, married Naoshige's father, Nabeshima Kiyofusa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Takanobu was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okitanawate">Battle of Okitanawate</a> in 1584, the change of power was gradual. His son Ryūzōji Masaie stayed head of the family, and when Masaie retired in 1590 his five-year-old son Takafusa succeeded him, while Naoshige governed the lands in fact. Only after Takafusa and Masaie both died in 1607 did Naoshige's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-katsushige">Nabeshima Katsushige</a> take the former Ryūzōji position as the first lord of Saga.</p><p>Under Katsushige and his successors three branch houses came to rule domains of their own, Ogi, Hasunoike and Kashima, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> recognised while Saga treated their lords as subordinate to the main house. Disputes over their status under the second lord, Nabeshima Mitsushige, led to the Sanke kakushiki regulations of 1683. Former Ryūzōji retainers and related families kept their own estates inside the domain throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naomasa">Nabeshima Naomasa</a>, lord from 1830, reformed the domain's finances and administration, expanded its school, the Kōdōkan, and strengthened the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>. From 1850 Saga built reverberatory furnaces for casting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and promoted Western medicine and education. Saga forces fought for the new imperial government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> of 1868–1869.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>With the abolition of the han system in 1871 the four domains became prefectures. The last lord, Nabeshima Naohiro, studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, served as Japanese minister plenipotentiary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1880 and sat in the House of Peers; under the Peerage Ordinance of 1884 the head of the house was made a marquess. The family remained in the peerage until the kazoku was abolished in 1947. A foundation set up in 1940 by Marquess Nabeshima Naomitsu runs the Chokokan museum in Saga, which keeps the family's documents and works of art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nabeshima clan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
